WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.431
A Bash script should invoke the Azure CLI
in a predictable and reviewable way.

2
00:00:07.511 --> 00:00:14.868
The safer pattern is a fixed command
structure with clearly separated options and

3
00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:15.908
operands.

4
00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:23.345
Avoid building one large command string
from variable data and asking the shell to

5
00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:25.238
interpret it again.

6
00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:31.182
That style makes quoting harder, hides
behavior from reviewers,

7
00:00:31.262 --> 00:00:36.807
and can produce surprising results when
input is unexpected.

8
00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:44.031
Quote values that should remain literal
and use arrays where the script needs to

9
00:00:44.111 --> 00:00:46.990
assemble options and operands.

10
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:54.000
Validate command groups, operation names,
resource IDs, subscription IDs,

11
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.798
resource group names, regions, resource
names, file paths, output paths,

12
00:01:00.878 --> 00:01:04.503
filters, and timeout values before use.

13
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:12.367
Validation should favor expected formats,
approved scopes, and known target values.

14
00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:19.378
Working directory control matters when
scripts read templates, artifacts,

15
00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:25.109
policy files, deployment parameters, or
JSON configuration.

16
00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:30.627
A relative path should resolve from the
intended directory,

17
00:01:30.707 --> 00:01:34.545
not wherever the script happened to start.

18
00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:42.089
CLI version expectations also matter
because output formats, supported options,

19
00:01:42.169 --> 00:01:48.247
and service behavior can vary across tool
versions and extensions.

20
00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:52.272
Safe stopping is part of secure
automation.

21
00:01:52.352 --> 00:01:55.444
Scripts should check exit status,

22
00:01:55.524 --> 00:02:00.216
distinguish expected empty results from
failures,

23
00:02:00.296 --> 00:02:05.094
and stop before later steps trust
incomplete output.

24
00:02:05.174 --> 00:02:12.531
Clear errors help operators recover, but
those errors should not expose secrets,

25
00:02:12.611 --> 00:02:18.689
raw tokens, sensitive role details, or
excessive response bodies.

26
00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:25.060
A good Azure CLI wrapper is strict,
understandable, and easy to audit.
